<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hākau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1500</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hakau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hākau was ruler (aliʻi nui) of the island of Hawaiʻi around 1500, the son and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liloa">Līloa</a>. Hawaiian tradition portrays him as cruel and as having mistreated his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umi-a-liloa">ʻUmi-a-Līloa</a> and the priests and chiefs of his father's court. ʻUmi led a revolt with their support, killed Hākau at Waipiʻo and became ruler in his place.</p></div>
